    "case_summary": "A 30-year-old male dairy farm worker presented with multiple painless nodules on the fingers of both hands for 1 month. Seven to ten days after the initial nodules appeared, he developed vesicles that ruptured and formed hemorrhagic crusts. He also noted discomfort and pain along the medial aspect of his right arm and forearm. He denied trauma or insect bites but reported that his cow had similar lesions on its teats.\n\nOn examination, 3–4 firm, non-tender nodules with central hemorrhagic crusts and surrounding erythema were present on the proximal phalanges of both hands. There was mild tenderness and increased warmth along the medial right arm and forearm.\n\nDermoscopy (10×) of one nodule showed an erythematous area with central ulceration and crust, yellow-white streaks, brown dots, a structureless whitish area partially surrounding the ulcer, an erythematous ring, and dot vessels.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Milker's nodules\nB. Milker’s nodules was most consistent with a bovine\nC. Orf was considered because it presents with clinically and histopathologically similar lesions due to a parapoxvirus\nD. Anthrax, atypical mycobacteriosis, and tularemia were also in the differential diagnosis but were less likely given the\nE. None\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
